@startuml agent-builder-phase3-flow
' ============================================================================
'  AGENT BUILDER - PHASE 3 (validate + reload)
'  Read the ACT brackets down the middle; the notes on the right explain, in
'  plain english, what is happening and why.
'  Render (from this folder):
'    java -jar ../../../../plantuml.jar -tpng agent-builder-phase3-flow.puml
' ============================================================================

skinparam backgroundColor #FAFAFA
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultFontName "Segoe UI"
skinparam defaultFontSize 12
skinparam ArrowColor #555555
skinparam ArrowFontColor #444444
skinparam ArrowFontSize 11
skinparam ActivityBorderColor #6A6A6A
skinparam ActivityBackgroundColor #FFFFFF
skinparam ActivityDiamondBackgroundColor #FFF4E5
skinparam ActivityDiamondBorderColor #C9A227
skinparam NoteBackgroundColor #FFFDF0
skinparam NoteBorderColor #C9B458
skinparam NoteFontSize 12
skinparam partitionBorderColor #9AA0B5
skinparam partitionFontStyle bold
skinparam partitionFontSize 13

title Agent Builder - PHASE 3: validate + reload\nThe story: the model wrote some files. Now we MARK them, then we MAKE THEM REAL.

start

group ACT 0 . Where we are when phase 3 begins
  partition "already done in phases 1 + 2" #EDE7F6 {
    :model read the spec, then wrote files with `write`\nagents/<id>/ : agent.toml, IDENTITY.md, skills/, ui/;
    :model called create_tool(agent=<id>)\n-> agents/<id>/plugins/<pid>/;
  }
  note right
    **PLAIN ENGLISH**
    The model has just finished writing a pile of files.
    Think of it as homework handed in, but nobody has
    marked it yet.

    Three things are wrong at this moment:
      1. Nothing has checked the files are correct.
      2. agent.toml is not live - the daemon read the
         agent list at startup and still has the old one.
      3. Your sidebar has no idea this agent exists.

    Phase 3 fixes exactly these three.
  end note
end group

group ACT 1 . MARK THE HOMEWORK  (validate_agent)
  partition "the model asks for a check  <<NEW>>" #C8E6C9 {
    :model calls **validate_agent**{agent_id};
    :ValidateAgentTool -> ValidateAgentService.validate();
  }

  partition "we do NOT re-invent the rules  <<NEW>>" #C8E6C9 {
    :**AgentDirReader** hands the folder to the CORE parser;
  }

  partition "the same parser the daemon uses at boot" #F3E8FF {
    :**FileAgentRegistry._load_dir()**\nagent.toml -> AgentSpec;
  }
  note right
    **PLAIN ENGLISH**
    First question: does this folder even load?

    We deliberately do NOT write our own agent.toml
    reader. We hand the folder to the exact same code
    the daemon runs at startup. If it produces an
    AgentSpec, the folder is structurally fine.

    Why it matters: two readers would drift apart, and
    then "valid" would mean two different things.
  end note

  if (did it parse?) then (no)
    :Finding(error, "agent.toml is broken / has no name");
  else (yes)
  endif

  partition "three sets of extra rules  <<NEW>>" #C8E6C9 {
    :**AgentLayoutRules** - is it built correctly?\ndisplay keys NOT inside [app]\n[app] holds only its five keys\nskills/ and plugins/ shaped right;
    :**PackageabilityRules** - can it ship as an .exe?\nhas [app], has version,\nui/ entry exists, nothing vital in workspace/;
    :**SandboxRules** - will its tools actually run?\nanything in agents/<id>/plugins/ is UNTRUSTED\nwarn if it needs network or API keys;
    :collect everything -> **Report**;
  }
  note right
    **PLAIN ENGLISH - why three separate rule sets**

    The parser only answers "does it load". These answer
    "is it any GOOD", and they are three different
    questions:

    * LAYOUT - things the daemon shrugs at but that are
      still wrong. Real example: expense-summarizer had
      colour/tagline/suggestions inside [app]. Perfectly
      valid TOML, silently ignored, so the agent showed a
      purple dot instead of the green one that was written.
      Nobody noticed for weeks.

    * PACKAGEABLE - things that only explode LATER, when
      you run the installer build. Better to hear it now
      than five minutes into gen:app.

    * SANDBOX - a tool the agent owns is treated as
      untrusted code (see the note further down). If it
      needs an API key it will quietly get nothing. The
      author should learn that while writing it, not in
      production.
  end note
end group

group ACT 2 . THE FIX LOOP  (this is the whole point)
  partition "findings go back to the model" #EDE7F6 {
    :ToolResult.text(Report.as_text());
  }

  if (any errors?) then (yes)
    partition "model repairs its own work" #EDE7F6 {
      :model reads each finding (file + what is wrong);
      :model calls **write** to fix it;
    }
    note right
      **PLAIN ENGLISH**
      This loop is the actual value of phase 3.

      Without it, a mistake shows up minutes later at
      build time - or never, silently, like the purple-dot
      bug did.

      With it, the model gets told "line X is wrong,
      here is why", fixes it, and asks again - all inside
      the same turn, before you ever see it.
    end note
    -[#C9A227]-> check again;
  else (no - clean)
  endif
end group

group ACT 3 . MAKE IT REAL  (reload_agent)
  partition "the model asks to activate it  <<NEW>>" #C8E6C9 {
    :model calls **reload_agent**{agent_id};
    :ReloadAgentService -> RegistryReloadAdapter;
  }

  partition "re-read the definition + find new tools" #F3E8FF {
    :**FileAgentRegistry.refresh() / add()**\nre-read agent.toml -> fresh AgentSpec;
    :**register_plugin_live()**\nre-scans agents/*/plugins/ from scratch\nand replaces the private-tool map;
  }
  note right
    **PLAIN ENGLISH**
    The daemon caches two things: the agent DEFINITION
    and its PRIVATE TOOLS. Writing files does not update
    either, so we ask it to re-read them.

    Note what is NOT here:
      * skills - re-read on every single turn already
      * ui/    - served straight off disk every request
    Those are live the moment you save. Only these two
    needed a nudge, which is why this tool is small.
  end note

  partition "trust check on the agent's own tools" #FFF4E5 {
    :**classify_origin()**\ntool came from agents/<id>/plugins/\n=> THIRD_PARTY_BUNDLE (untrusted);
    if (sandboxing switched on?) then (yes)
      :wrap it: no secrets, no network by default;
    else (no - today's default)
      :runs normally, in-process;
    endif
  }
  note right
    **PLAIN ENGLISH - the "third party" thing**
    An agent can be DOWNLOADED from the marketplace, and
    it can carry its own Python code inside it. That code
    would then run on your machine with your API keys.

    So the rule is by LOCATION, not by author: anything
    under agents/<id>/plugins/ is treated as a stranger's
    code. It cannot tell "I wrote this by chat" apart from
    "I installed this from the store".

    That folder is exactly where phase 2 writes. So your
    own tools get treated as untrusted too. Switched OFF
    by default today - but this is why SandboxRules warns
    you up front.
  end note
end group

group ACT 4 . TELL EVERYONE
  partition "announce the change  <<MODIFIED>>" #FFE0B2 {
    :**Gateway.broadcast_agents_changed()**\n(new: pulled out of _agents_create so a\nplugin can trigger the same announcement);
    :`agents.changed` sent to every open window;
  }
  note right
    **PLAIN ENGLISH**
    The agent now works - but your sidebar was drawn
    before it existed, so it still looks empty.

    The daemon already knows how to shout "the agent list
    changed" (the + button uses it). We just need a plugin
    to be able to shout the same thing.

    That is the ONE change to core code in this phase:
    give plugins a handle to that shout.
  end note

  partition "the client is already listening" #E8F0FE {
    :store.ts `gateway.on('agents.changed')`\n-> sidebar redraws;
  }
}

group ACT 5 . YOU CAN NOW USE IT
  partition "test it for real" #E0F2F1 {
    :open a NEW chat with the new agent\n(runs alongside the builder chat - the lock is per chat);
    :or click it open as its own app window (if it has ui/);
  }
  note right
    **PLAIN ENGLISH**
    You describe -> it builds -> it checks itself ->
    it goes live -> you try it in a second chat ->
    you come back and say what is wrong.

    When you are happy:
      build-agent-installer.sh <id>   ->  a shippable .exe
  end note
end group

stop

legend left
  | Colour | Meaning |
  |<#C8E6C9> | **NEW in phase 3** - the plugins/agent-builder/ bundle |
  |<#FFE0B2> | **MODIFIED** - Gateway + PluginContext + container (one additive field) |
  |<#EDE7F6> | Existing - the model's tool-calling loop |
  |<#F3E8FF> | Existing - registry, parser, hot-reload |
  |<#FFF4E5> | Existing - sandbox trust classification |
  |<#E8F0FE> | Existing - desktop client (needs NO work this phase) |
  |<#E0F2F1> | The outcome |
endlegend

@enduml
